  "text": "Solution 1 is more likely correct, though I could not check the literature here, so this rests on recall rather than verification.\n\n- **Degree ≤ 4 is settled.** Braun–Oréfice-Okamoto (2016) proved that a nowhere-vanishing Jacobian with deg p ≤ 4 forces global injectivity. Both solutions agree on this.\n- **Degree 5 is where they disagree.** Solution 1 cites a result that deg p ≤ 5 suffices, which I believe is a Braun–Fernandes-type theorem on degree-5 submersions. Solution 2 instead claims degree-5 counterexamples exist and attributes them to the same authors. These cannot both be right, and the attribution in Solution 2 seems to be misremembered.\n- **Sharpness at degree 6.** Solution 1 asserts non-injective examples with deg p = 6. I cannot confirm this from memory. Pinchuk's original example has degrees 10 and 25, and I do not reliably recall a degree-6 counterexample. If it does not exist, the true maximum would be larger than 5 and neither solution would be correct.\n\nGiven the uncertainty about the degree-6 claim, Solution 1 is the better candidate, but I would not treat its final answer as verified.\n\nCHOICE: 1",
